  planner_response {
    response: "I have reviewed the master portfolio specification and your uploaded portrait (`/public/assets/aistudio/image_7a10922a.png`). To tailor the portfolio specifically to you, please answer these quick questions (you can also use the write-in option on any question to provide your exact full name, location, or bio)."
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Request**\n\nI\'m focusing now on the user\'s explicit instructions: \"follow the file and ask me questions about me. implement images.\" The image file suggests a professional context, and my current thought is how to incorporate this visual information and the \"universal_portfolio_prompt__\" into this process. I\'m considering best approaches to asking the user appropriate questions, and how to use the image effectively.\n\n\n**Refining Identity Extraction**\n\nNow I\'m diving deeper into the configuration schema. I am examining each field within the `identity` section, particularly `fullName`, `displayName`, `profession`, `headline`, and `about`. I\'m starting to build out a mental map of the key questions I need to ask the user, tailored to extract the necessary information to populate these fields. The image file is clearly providing cues for a particular professional context. I\'m focusing on how to guide the user towards effective self-description, using the specified fields as a framework.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Question Strategy**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the question-asking phase, focusing on how to elicit the necessary information based on the \"Caveat\" font and \"balanced\" animation intensity in the provided data, and the user\'s instructions. I\'ll need to use the `ask_question` tool, presenting 2-4 multiple-choice questions to determine the user\'s preferences effectively. I need to be sure to yield after asking.\n\n\n**Implementing Question Format**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the formatting rules for the `ask_question` tool. I\'m verifying the \'plain text only\' constraint for questions and options, ensuring conciseness (1-2 sentences) and brevity for each choice (under 10 words). Multiple-choice options, ranging from 2-6, will be prioritized. I\'ve also noted that recommended options must be listed first, and there is no need to add an \"Other\" option. I\'m preparing to formulate questions, and I\'m mindful of the immediate yield. I\'m also confirming the user\'s name is likely Siful Abdullah.\n\n\n**Formulating Core Questions**\n\nI\'m now writing focused multiple-choice questions per the schema. I\'m prioritizing clarity and brevity in questions. I am framing options to determine the user\'s primary profession and showcase categories. I am drafting questions for determining professional style and availability. I have also decided on which options to present. I am keeping in mind the need to yield after the question.\n\n\n**Drafting Multiple-Choice Questions**\n\nI\'m finalizing the question set. I\'ve composed focused multiple-choice questions for the user. I\'ve carefully crafted multiple-choice questions for each question, including four distinct questions about the user\'s primary focus and style. I\'ve ensured clarity and relevance in the options for each question, using the provided example. These questions are designed to extract critical information about their preferred presentation and project showcase, for example, questions 3 and 4.\n\n\n"
