  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Implementation**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the user\'s request. My priority is to understand the physics of particle-to-particle collisions and how to translate that into a suitable technical design within the AntigravityCanvas component. I\'m carefully evaluating the technical aspects of implementation, thinking about computational costs and performance optimizations to make sure this is efficient.\n\n\n**Implementing Physical Collisions**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of particle collisions. My focus is on implementing physically accurate, elastic bounces. The core logic involves calculating distances, setting a collision threshold, and then determining the normal vector and overlap when particles interact. I need to figure out the right collision padding for a good visual and physical experience. I have formulas prepared for each of these.\n\n\n**Refining Collision Mechanics**\n\nI\'m presently zeroing in on the collision response, specifically the impulse calculation for elastic bounces. I\'m focusing on the positional separation to prevent sticking by pushing the particles apart along the normal vector, using the overlap. I\'m calculating relative velocity and adjusting it with the restitution coefficient to produce a realistic kinetic bounce. I\'m also planning to add a visual impact element, like a brief visual effect that decays smoothly.\n\n\n**Optimizing Performance Now**\n\nI\'m now deeply invested in performance optimization, given the particle count. A brute-force approach quickly becomes inefficient. I\'ve been evaluating spatial partitioning techniques, specifically grid hashing, to drastically reduce collision checks. The plan is to implement a grid with a cell size of around 40px, enabling me to significantly optimize the system. This method promises a buttery smooth 60 FPS even on mobile!\n\n\n**Decoupling and Testing**\n\nI\'m now separating the collision physics into `src/utils/portfolio.ts`, with a dedicated function `resolveParticleCollision`. I\'m also preparing unit tests for this function in `src/utils/portfolio.test.ts` to ensure its accuracy. I plan to use `npx --yes tsx --test` to verify my unit tests.\n\n\n**Validating Elastic Impulse**\n\nMy focus is now on meticulously evaluating the elastic impulse calculation and collision resolution logic I developed. I\'m verifying the accuracy of the positional correction and impulse application, particularly when the particles are interacting under mouse influence. I\'m testing edge cases to guarantee that the physics remain consistent even with the cursor sweeping through the particles.\n\n\n**Adding Visual Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now integrating visual effects to enhance the particle collisions, focusing on feedback and aesthetics. I\'m adding `collisionEnergy` to particles for crimson bounce rings. When a collision occurs, this energy increases. Also, the physical \"wave\" effect from dense clusters is really coming into focus. I need to make sure to update tests after adding the physics to the code.\n\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
      id: "call_6559832239134593244"
      name: "replace_file_content"
      arguments_json: "{\"AllowMultiple\":false, \"Description\":\"Add CollidableParticle interface and resolveParticleCollision utility function\", \"EndLine\":146, \"Instruction\":\"Export CollidableParticle interface and resolveParticleCollision function with restitution and positional correction\", \"ReplacementContent\":\"  vx *= friction;\\n  vy *= friction;\\n\\n  return {\\n    ...particle,\\n    x: particle.x + vx,\\n    y: particle.y + vy,\\n    vx,\\n    vy,\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nexport interface CollidableParticle {\\n  x: number;\\n  y: number;\\n  vx: number;\\n  vy: number;\\n  radius: number;\\n  mass?: number;\\n}\\n\\n/**\\n * Resolves an elastic or semi-elastic collision between two 2D particles.\\n * Returns true if a collision was detected and resolved.\\n */\\nexport function resolveParticleCollision(\\n  p1: CollidableParticle,\\n  p2: CollidableParticle,\\n  restitution: number = 0.85,\\n  spacingMargin: number = 2.0\\n): boolean {\\n  const dx = p2.x - p1.x;\\n  const dy = p2.y - p1.y;\\n  const distSq = dx * dx + dy * dy;\\n  const minDist = p1.radius + p2.radius + spacingMargin;\\n\\n  if (distSq >= minDist * minDist || distSq <= 0.0001) {\\n    return false;\\n  }\\n\\n  const dist = Math.sqrt(distSq);\\n  const nx = dx / dist;\\n  const ny = dy / dist;\\n\\n  // Positional correction to resolve penetration\\n  const overlap = (minDist - dist) * 0.5;\\n  p1.x -= nx * overlap;\\n  p1.y -= ny * overlap;\\n  p2.x += nx * overlap;\\n  p2.y += ny * overlap;\\n\\n  // Relative velocity along collision normal\\n  const rvx = p2.vx - p1.vx;\\n  const rvy = p2.vy - p1.vy;\\n  const velAlongNormal = rvx * nx + rvy * ny;\\n\\n  // Do not resolve impulse if particles are already separating\\n  if (velAlongNormal > 0) {\\n    return true;\\n  }\\n\\n  const m1 = p1.mass || (p1.radius * p1.radius);\\n  const m2 = p2.mass || (p2.radius * p2.radius);\\n\\n  // Impulse scalar\\n  const impulse = (-(1 + restitution) * velAlongNormal) / (1 / m1 + 1 / m2);\\n\\n  p1.vx -= (impulse / m1) * nx;\\n  p1.vy -= (impulse / m1) * ny;\\n  p2.vx += (impulse / m2) * nx;\\n  p2.vy += (impulse / m2) * ny;\\n\\n  return true;\\n}\\n\", \"StartLine\":135, \"TargetContent\":\"  vx *= friction;\\n  vy *= friction;\\n\\n  return {\\n    ...particle,\\n    x: particle.x + vx,\\n    y: particle.y + vy,\\n    vx,\\n    vy,\\n  };\\n}\", \"TargetFile\":\"/src/utils/portfolio.ts\", \"toolAction\":\"Updating portfolio utils\", \"toolSummary\":\"Add particle collision physics utility\"}"
